  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ILO led its first RBSA project in Eritrea with focus on Occupational Safety and health and formalisation in 2021. The project was successfully implemented with delivery rate of 84%. The main partner organisation was National Confederation for Eritrean Workers (NCEW). The project played an important role in building awareness among workers on OSH during Covid-19 and paved the way for the publication of Eritrea Labour Survey with a formalisation diagnostic study. However, in the president of NCEW unexpectedly passed away. The change of NCEW charismatic leadership hindered a new project to take place and built on the results of the RBSA project. 

The below interventions are based on the results achieved throughout the RBSA project on Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) and formalisation that was implemented in Eritrea in 2021, and furtherly requested by NCEW: 

Indicator 2.2.2: Number of workers organisations to produce proposals considered in social dialogue mechanisms for policy making. 

&#x2022;	Support an awareness tripartite webinar on principles of OSH embedded in C155 and C187.  
&#x2022;	Support NCEW to develop Advocacy Ratification Campaign for C155 and C187. 
&#x2022;	NCEW participate the African Regional Academy on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work (FPRW), 18 &#x2013; 22 NOVEMBER 2024, in KIGALI, RWANDA.</narrative>
  </description>
